    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Differentiate under the integral sign",
        "body": "Let $I(a)=\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{\\sin(ax)}xe^{-x^2}dx$, $I(0)=0$. Then $I'(a)=\\int_0^\\infty\\cos(ax)e^{-x^2}dx=\\frac{\\sqrt\\pi}2e^{-a^2/4}$, so $I(a)=\\frac\\pi2\\operatorname{erf}\\frac a2$. The required integral is $I(\\sqrt3)-I(1/\\sqrt3)=\\frac\\pi2\\left(\\operatorname{erf}\\frac{\\sqrt3}2-\\operatorname{erf}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt3}\\right)\\approx0.72637$ (confirmed by numerical integration)."
