    "answerDisplay": "$\\ln2$",
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Solution",
        "body": "Since $\\tan(\\arctan x+\\arctan(1-x))=\\frac{1}{1-x(1-x)}=\\frac1{x^2-x+1}$, we have $\\arctan\\frac1{x^2-x+1}=\\arctan x+\\arctan(1-x)$, hence $\\arctan(x^2-x+1)=\\frac\\pi2-\\arctan x-\\arctan(1-x)$. Integrating over $[0,1]$ (the two arctan terms have equal integrals): $\\frac\\pi2-2\\int_0^1\\arctan x\\,dx=\\frac\\pi2-2\\left(\\frac\\pi4-\\frac{\\ln2}2\\right)=\\ln2$."
      }
